ash as a Flatpak
================

This package is built and tested here and published as a GitHub Release asset. It is
not submitted to Flathub, and nothing under packaging/flatpak/ talks to Flathub except
to download the org.freedesktop runtime it builds against.

What you get on PATH, which is not "ash"
----------------------------------------

The .deb and .rpm both install /usr/bin/ash. A Flatpak cannot: /app is a read-only
OSTree checkout, and the only host-visible name flatpak exports is the application ID.
So after installing you have

  flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper scan --source-dir .

and the app ID, not "ash", is what appears in
/var/lib/flatpak/exports/bin. All three names the wheel declares are inside the
sandbox, reachable with --command:

  flatpak run --command=ash                       io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper --version
  flatpak run --command=ashv3                     io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper --version
  flatpak run --command=automated-security-helper io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper --version

ash is canonical. ashv3 warns once on stderr and then runs. automated-security-helper
is silent and kept indefinitely, for hosts where a bare ash resolves to something else.
Because "ash" is the default command, the first form above is what a bare
`flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper` gives you.

For a short name, alias it or link it yourself:

  # a shell alias
  alias ash='flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper'

  # or a wrapper on PATH, which also works from scripts and hooks
  printf '#!/bin/sh\nexec flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper "$@"\n' \
    | sudo tee /usr/local/bin/ash >/dev/null && sudo chmod 0755 /usr/local/bin/ash

The sandbox trade: this package grants --filesystem=host
--------------------------------------------------------

Read this section before deciding whether to use the Flatpak rather than the .deb, the
.rpm, or a pip install.

ASH reads arbitrary source trees, and it writes its report inside the tree it scanned.
Concretely, in automated_security_helper/cli/scan.py: --source-dir defaults to the
process working directory, and --output-dir defaults to <source-dir>/.ash/ash_output.
Both are host paths the user picks at invocation time.

A Flatpak with no filesystem grant therefore installs cleanly, runs `ash --version`
cleanly, and then scans nothing. It does not error: the sandbox shows it an empty
directory where the source tree should be, and an empty scan exits 0 with a report
containing no findings. That is the worst available outcome -- a security tool that
reports all-clear because it could not see the code.

So the manifest grants --filesystem=host. What that gives up, stated plainly: the app
can read and write every top-level path under / and everything below them, except the
reserved set (/app, /bin, /dev, /etc, /lib, /lib32, /lib64, /proc, /run/flatpak,
/run/host, /sbin, /usr) and except /boot, /efi, /root, /sys, /tmp, /var and
~/.var/app, which "host" does not cover. Your home directory, your SSH keys, your
cloud credentials, and every other project on the machine are all inside that. This is
most of the filesystem confinement a Flatpak would otherwise give you.

What remains: process, IPC and network-namespace separation, an immutable /app, a
runtime that is not your host's /usr, and no display, audio or D-Bus access at all --
the manifest grants no --socket and no --talk-name. Those are real, and they are not
what most people mean by "sandboxed".

Why nothing narrower works
--------------------------

  --filesystem=home        Covers a checkout under $HOME and nothing else. /srv, /opt,
                           /workspace, /data and a second disk are all ordinary places
                           to keep source, and none of them is under $HOME.

  --filesystem=/path       Static. Flatpak grants permit no variable expansion or
                           substitution, so there is no way to write "wherever the user
                           cd'd to" -- which is exactly what ASH's default source
                           directory is.

  the Documents portal     A portal hands an app one file the user picked in a dialog.
                           ASH takes a directory on a command line, walks it itself,
                           and is run non-interactively from pre-commit hooks and CI
                           where there is no dialog and no user to answer it.

  --persist=DIR            Not available to this app at all: --persist only works when
                           the app has no home-or-broader permission, and host is
                           broader. It would not have solved the source-tree problem
                           anyway; it redirects a path in the sandbox to the app's own
                           data directory rather than exposing a host path.

If you want tighter confinement
-------------------------------

The grant in the manifest is a default, not a ceiling. flatpak override changes it
per machine, and the narrower grant then applies to every invocation:

  # Replace whole-host access with one tree
  flatpak override --user \
    --nofilesystem=host \
    --filesystem=~/src \
    io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

  # Inspect what is actually in force
  flatpak info --show-permissions io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

  # Undo it
  flatpak override --user --reset io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

With that override, ASH can scan anything under ~/src and nothing else -- and a scan
pointed anywhere else reports zero findings rather than an error, for the reason at the
top of this section. Add --filesystem=:ro if you also pass --output-dir to somewhere
writable; the default output path is inside the scanned tree, so a read-only grant on
that tree alone is not enough.

If filesystem confinement is the reason you are considering a Flatpak, the honest
answer is that this package is the wrong tool for it. A .deb, an .rpm, or a pip install
inside a container you control gives you the same scanning behavior with confinement
you can reason about, and packaging/README.md points at both.

Container mode does not work from inside this Flatpak
-----------------------------------------------------

`ash scan --mode container` cannot work here, and this is measured rather than assumed:
the verification script asserts it.

ASH's container runner looks for finch, docker, nerdctl or podman on PATH and shells
out to whichever it finds first (automated_security_helper/interactions/
run_ash_container.py). Inside the sandbox, PATH is /app/bin:/usr/bin, and that /usr is
the runtime's own -- /usr is a reserved path that even --filesystem=host does not
expose. A runner installed on the host is not on PATH and cannot be put there.

Granting the docker socket would not fix this and is not worth attempting. It would
need the client binary injected into the sandbox as well as the socket, and write access
to a container daemon's socket is equivalent to root on the host, so it would remove
every remaining protection this package has rather than restoring one feature.

The default mode is local (RunMode.local), so this affects only an explicit
`--mode container`. Local mode is the mode this package is verified in. `--mode nix` is
unavailable for the same reason: it needs a host binary the sandbox cannot see.

If you need container mode, use the .deb, the .rpm, or a pip install on the host.

Why this package needs a Python index on first run
--------------------------------------------------

The Flatpak bundles ASH's own wheel and nothing else. There is no post-install hook in
Flatpak -- an installed app is immutable and no code runs on your machine at install
time -- so instead of a %post scriptlet, the launcher at /app/bin/ash creates a
virtualenv the first time you run it, under

  ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/data/

and installs that wheel into it, which resolves ASH's 27 runtime dependencies from
whatever Python index the host is configured for. Later runs find the venv and exec it.

This is not an oversight. ASH's own code may ship in a package published by the
project; third-party code may not. Carrying exactly one wheel is the guard that keeps
this package on the right side of that line, because it can be checked by counting the
files under /app/share/ash/wheels/ rather than by judging each dependency one at a
time.

For a Flatpak the pressure to do otherwise is stronger than for the .deb or .rpm. The
conventional Python recipe is flatpak-pip-generator, which emits a sources list with one
URL and hash per dependency and a build step that pip-installs them into /app -- and a
bundle carries whatever is in /app, so that would publish 27 third-party distributions.
Two things make it fail rather than merely discourage it: build.sh passes
--disable-download, and no build-options grants the build sandbox network. Measured, by
adding a `pip download requests` to the manifest's build-commands:

  Failed to resolve 'pypi.org' ([Errno -3] Temporary failure in name resolution)

So a build-time dependency install cannot reach an index even if someone adds one.

Building a self-contained offline bundle for your own hosts is fine, and the commands
below are the supported way to do it. The line is about what the project publishes, not
about what the bytes are allowed to do.

On a host with no reachable index, stage a wheelhouse and point the first run at it:

  pip download automated-security-helper==<version> -d /srv/ash-wheels

  PIP_NO_INDEX=1 PIP_FIND_LINKS=/srv/ash-wheels \
    flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper --version

Environment variables and not /etc/pip.conf. The pip that runs is inside the sandbox,
which sees the runtime's /etc rather than the host's, so a host /etc/pip.conf is invisible
to it. Variables do cross: `PIP_NO_INDEX=1 flatpak run ...` reaches the first-run
bootstrap and stops it with "No matching distribution found for attrs<27.0.0,>=26.1",
which is how the two commands above were checked.

/srv is readable from the sandbox for the same reason a source tree is -- see the coverage
list below. /tmp and /var are not, so a wheelhouse in either is invisible.

For a setting that persists rather than being retyped, the pip config file the sandbox
actually reads is under the app's own config directory. Taken from
`pip config debug` run inside the sandbox, the user-level candidates are

  ~/.pip/pip.conf
  ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/config/pip/pip.conf

and the second is the one flatpak points $XDG_CONFIG_HOME at:

  mkdir -p ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/config/pip
  printf '[global]\nno-index = true\nfind-links = /srv/ash-wheels\n' \
    > ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/config/pip/pip.conf

Note that ~/.config/pip/pip.conf -- the path that works for a normal pip -- is NOT read,
because $XDG_CONFIG_HOME inside the sandbox is not ~/.config.

What --filesystem=host actually covers, measured
------------------------------------------------

Writing a marker file on the host and reading it back from inside the sandbox:

  /home    visible        /tmp   not visible
  /srv     visible        /var   not visible
  /opt     visible        /root  not visible
  /mnt     visible

A source tree under any of the visible paths scans normally. One under /var -- a CI
workspace at /var/lib/<something>/workspace, for instance -- does not, and the symptom is
a clean scan with no findings rather than an error. Grant it explicitly:

  flatpak override --user --filesystem=/var/lib/jenkins \
    io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

A write to a path the sandbox cannot see does not fail. It lands in the sandbox's own
throwaway filesystem, reads back correctly for the rest of that invocation, and is gone
afterwards -- measured by writing a file under an ungranted $HOME, seeing `ls -l` report
it, and finding it absent in the very next `flatpak run` and absent on the host. That is
the shape to expect from every ungranted path, and the reason the coverage list above is
worth checking against where your source and your output directory live.

Scanners are not installed by this package
------------------------------------------

ASH drives third-party scanners; it does not contain them. After installing:

  flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper dependencies install
  flatpak run io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper dependencies install --tool grype

ASH installs those into $ASH_BIN_PATH, which defaults to ~/.ash/bin. For an ordinary user
whose home is under /home that is a real host path, so the binaries land in the same place
the .deb and .rpm put them and a machine with both packages shares one set.

For root, whose home is /root, it is not: /root is outside --filesystem=host, so the
install appears to succeed and the binaries are gone by the next invocation -- see the
measured note at the end of the coverage section above. If you are running as root, or your
home is anywhere the coverage list does not include, either grant it

  flatpak override --user --filesystem=/root io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

or send the binaries somewhere already reachable

  ASH_BIN_PATH=/srv/ash-bin flatpak run \
    io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper dependencies install

That second form rests on two things: environment variables set outside `flatpak run` do
reach the app, and ASH reads $ASH_BIN_PATH in preference to the ~/.ash/bin default
(automated_security_helper/core/constants.py:19-22).

Why the runtime is org.freedesktop.Sdk and not org.freedesktop.Platform
----------------------------------------------------------------------

Platform is the conventional runtime for an application and it is the wrong one here.
Measured inside `flatpak run` against 24.08 of each: both ship Python 3.12.14 with
ensurepip, so the interpreter is a wash. Platform has no git and no compiler; Sdk has
both.

git matters because ASH shells out to it to compute a scan set, and the call site
returns None when git is absent -- which widens a diff-scoped or precommit scan to
scanning everything rather than failing. A scan whose scope quietly changed is worse
than one that refuses to start. A compiler matters because the first run installs 27
dependencies from an index, and any without a matching wheel are built from source.

The cost is size: about 1.8 GB installed against Platform's smaller footprint. If you
already have other Flatpaks, some of that is shared; if this is your only one, it is
not. Nothing else is downloaded, though -- the app names Sdk as both its runtime and
its sdk, so building it needs that one runtime rather than two.

Files
-----

  /app/share/ash/wheels/       ASH's wheel, the app's only payload
  /app/bin/ash                 the launcher; creates the venv on first run
  /app/bin/ashv3               symlink to ash, dispatching on basename
  /app/bin/automated-security-helper   symlink to ash

  ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/data/
                               where first run builds the venv, named after the wheel
                               and the python version

The venv path contains the wheel filename and the interpreter version on purpose. An
upgraded Flatpak carries a new wheel, so it gets a new venv rather than serving the old
ASH out of the old one -- there is no post-install hook to clear it, and a stale venv
would make `ash --version` report the previous release with no error anywhere. A new
runtime branch changes the python version the same way.

Old venvs stay behind after an upgrade. That is disk, not correctness:

  du -sh ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/data/*
  rm -rf ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper/data/automated_security_helper-<old>*

Removing the package
--------------------

  flatpak uninstall io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

leaves ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper alone, so the venv and
anything else the app wrote survive. This differs from the .deb and .rpm, whose removal
steps delete /usr/lib/ash/venv, and it is flatpak's design rather than a bug -- that
directory is user data. To remove the app and its data together:

  flatpak uninstall --delete-data io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

Use that form instead of the plain one, not after it. --delete-data acts on an installed
app: run against one you already uninstalled, it exits 1 with "No installed refs found"
and leaves the data where it was. If you have already uninstalled, delete the directory
directly:

  rm -rf ~/.var/app/io.github.awslabs.automated_security_helper

On a machine with no session bus -- a container, an SSH session with no display --
--delete-data removes the data and then exits non-zero with "Cannot autolaunch D-Bus
without X11 $DISPLAY", because it also tries to revoke the app's portal permissions over
that bus. The data is gone; check the directory rather than the exit code.

Scanner binaries under ~/.ash/bin are not touched by any of these, because ASH put them
there and no package manager tracks them.

Building it yourself
--------------------

  ./packaging/flatpak/build.sh dist/automated_security_helper-<version>-py3-none-any.whl out/

needs flatpak, flatpak-builder, and org.freedesktop.Sdk//24.08 installed. It writes
out/ash-<version>-<arch>.flatpak.

flatpak-builder drives bwrap, which has to create a user namespace, and an ordinary
Docker container does not permit that. Measured on a Docker 25.0 host:

  docker run fedora:41 bwrap --dev-bind / / --unshare-user-try /bin/true
    -> bwrap: No permissions to creating new namespace, likely because the kernel does
       not allow non-privileged user namespaces.                            (exit 1)
  docker run --privileged fedora:41 <the same>                              (exit 0)

That is why packaging/flatpak/verify-in-container.sh needs a privileged container while
the .deb and .rpm scripts run in an ordinary job container, and why the CI job for this
package runs on the runner host rather than under a `container:` key. The script checks
bwrap before it checks anything else, so an environment that cannot build a Flatpak says
so in its first few lines.

  docker run --rm --privileged -v "$PWD:/src" -v ash-flatpak-store:/var/lib/flatpak \
    fedora:41 bash /src/packaging/flatpak/verify-in-container.sh
